25, -0.01197052001953125, 0.0181884765625, -0.0210418701171875, 0.014495849609375, -0.005023956298828125, 0.0256805419921875, 0.0222625732421875, 0.033782958984375, 0.0026988983154296875, 0.0019407272338867188, -0.01263427734375, 0.0135650634765625, 0.0272216796875, -0.022216796875, -0.00952911376953125, -0.03729248046875, -0.047760009765625, 0.019775390625, -0.00827789306640625, -0.003131866455078125, -0.0216217041015625, 0.0134124755859375, 0.0012369155883789062, 0.016754150390625, -0.01544189453125, -0.00745391845703125, -0.01255035400390625, 0.010528564453125, -0.003406524658203125, 0.014404296875, -0.0083770751953125, 0.005245208740234375, -0.021240234375, 0.003662109375, -0.005466461181640625, -0.005977630615234375, 0.0261688232421875, -0.015045166015625, 0.0123748779296875, -0.0248260498046875, -0.008087158203125, 0.0216217041015625, -0.005199432373046875, -0.0083465576171875, -0.0109100341796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. \n\n**Annotation Protocol and Quality Control**  \nIn this study, \"skilled accounting judgement\" was operationalised by having three professional accountants (each with at least five years of experience and relevant certifications) annotate each invoice according to a standardised set of GL coding guidelines. These guidelines were based on the client\u2019s chart of accounts and industry best practices, specifying, for example, that IT hardware purchases should be coded as \"IT Equipment\" unless the description or vendor indicates office supplies. Ambiguity was operationally defined as cases where the invoice description, vendor, or context did not clearly map to a single GL code, such as invoices with vague descriptions like \"services rendered\" or those from vendors supplying multiple categories. Annotators flagged such cases using a dedicated annotation field. \n\nFlagged invoices were discussed in weekly consensus meetings, where each annotator presented their rationale referencing the guidelines. Disagreements were resolved through structured discussion, followed by a majority vote if consensus was not reached. Approximately 10% of invoices underwent this process, and in cases where no majority emerged, a senior accountant acted as final arbiter. Across the dataset, the initial inter-annotator disagreement rate was 7.5%. Of the flagged cases, 82% reached consensus in the first meeting, 15% required a majority vote, and 3% were escalated to the senior accountant. Annotation quality was monitored by periodic spot checks (5% of all annotations per month), with a re-annotation rate below 2%. Inter-annotator agreement was measured on a subset of 1,000 invoices, yielding a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.89, indicating high consistency.\n\n**Background and Definitions**  \nRecent advances in natural language processing have enabled the use of sentence transformers and small language models (SLMs) for document classification. In this context, \"anisotropic\" refers to an embedding space where data points are unevenly distributed, while \"isotropic clusters\" are locally dense, uniform groupings within that space. \"Structured input\" denotes invoice data reformatted with explicit fields and labels, as opposed to unstructured, free-text input.\n\n**Dataset Characteristics**  \nWe analysed a proprietary financial corpus of 12,000 invoices from multiple clients, each labelled with GL codes and vendor identities. The dataset spans invoices issued between 2018 and 2023. There are 37 unique GL codes, with a long-tailed distribution: the top 5 codes account for 48% of invoices, while 12 codes have fewer than 50 examples each. Vendor frequency is similarly skewed: the top 10 vendors account for 35% of invoices, and 60% of vendors appear fewer than 10 times. Annotation was performed by three certified accountants following the documented protocol above, with periodic adjudication to ensure label quality.\n\nPreprocessing included anonymisation (removal or hashing of client and personal identifiers, but vendor names were retained as they are essential features), tokenisation, and removal of non-informative fields\u2014defined as fields with missing values in over 95% of invoices or containing only boilerplate text (e.g., page numbers, generic footers). The removal of these fields was justified to reduce noise and focus the model on relevant content; however, we acknowledge that some contextual cues may have been lost. For model evaluation, we used 5-fold cross-validation with client-level splits: invoices from each client appeared in only one fold, ensuring that test data represented unseen clients and preventing data leakage.\n\n**Model Selection and Baselines**  \nWe evaluated two pre-trained models: SBERT-base (~110M parameters) and DeBERTa-base (~140M parameters), both transformer-based SLMs that balance efficiency and strong performance on sentence-level tasks. These models were selected based on prior benchmarking studies (Reimers & Gurevych, 2019; He et al., 2021), which demonstrate their competitive performance on text classification and semantic similarity tasks, especially in resource-constrained settings. While smaller distilled models exist, preliminary ablation experiments on a 2,000-invoice subset showed that SBERT-base and DeBERTa-base outperformed distilled alternatives (e.g., DistilBERT) by 3\u20135 F1 points. Both models were fine-tuned on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU for 10 epochs, using a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of 2e-5.\n\nBaselines included a zero-shot large language model (LLM; GPT-3.5, prompted without fine-tuning) and a vendor identity baseline (assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor). The choice of GPT-3.5 zero-shot reflects a practical comparison point for organisations considering external LLM APIs versus in-house SLMs, despite its lack of domain adaptation. The vendor identity baseline serves as a simple heuristic reflecting common industry practice.\n\n**Geometric Analysis of Embedding Spaces**  \nTo assess embedding space geometry, we computed the isotropy coefficient (as in Ethayarajh, 2019), which measures the uniformity of embedding vector directions (values closer to 1 indicate uniformity, while values near 0 indicate concentration along certain directions). We also used silhouette scores (which quantify how similar an object is to its own cluster compared to other clusters; higher values indicate better separation) and Davies-Bouldin indices (which measure average similarity between clusters; lower values indicate more distinct clusters). Embeddings were visualised using t-SNE and UMAP to inspect cluster structure.\n\nThe sentence-embedding space was globally anisotropic (isotropy coefficient < 0.3), but contained locally isotropic clusters, with clusters strongly correlated to vendor identity (average silhouette score 0.62; Davies-Bouldin index 0.88). This indicates that the models learn vendor-specific representations, which may aid classification but also risk overfitting to vendor patterns. In practice, high silhouette scores for vendor clusters suggest that the model can easily distinguish invoices from frequent vendors, but may struggle to generalise to rare vendors or new clients. Visualisations (not shown here due to space) confirmed tight clusters for frequent vendors and more diffuse clusters for rare vendors. The observed geometry suggests that while the models capture meaningful structure for frequent vendors, generalisation to rare vendors or new contexts may be limited, highlighting the importance of monitoring for vendor drift and evaluating on unseen clients.\n\n**Results**  \nSBERT fine-tuned on our dataset achieved an average accuracy of 0.96 (\u00b10.01), F1 score of 0.94 (\u00b10.02), precision of 0.95, and recall of 0.93. DeBERTa-base achieved similar but slightly lower metrics (accuracy 0.95, F1 0.92). The zero-shot LLM baseline yielded accuracy 0.85, F1 0.81, precision 0.82, and recall 0.80. The vendor identity baseline achieved accuracy 0.88, F1 0.83, precision 0.84, and recall 0.82. McNemar\u2019s test indicated that SBERT\u2019s improvement over baselines was statistically significant (p < 0.01).\n\n**Granular Performance Analysis**  \nPerformance varied by GL code frequency and vendor frequency. For the top 5 most frequent GL codes, SBERT achieved per-class F1 scores between 0.95 and 0.97. For rare GL codes (support < 50), per-class F1 ranged from 0.78 to 0.87. Table 1 summarises per-class F1 for selected frequent and rare codes. For vendors with >100 invoices, F1 averaged 0.96; for vendors with <10 invoices, F1 dropped to 0.85. For new clients (not seen during training), SBERT reached an F1 score of 0.90 (\u00b10.03) with approximately 100 client-specific invoices. Error analysis revealed that most misclassifications occurred in underrepresented GL codes and for invoices with ambiguous or minimal descriptions. The most common error types were confusion between similar expense categories (e.g., \"Office Supplies\" vs. \"IT Equipment\") and misclassification of invoices with vague descriptions.\n\n| GL Code Frequency | F1 Score (SBERT) |\n|-------------------|------------------|\n| Frequent (>500)   | 0.97             |\n| Moderate (100-500)| 0.92             |\n| Rare (<50)        | 0.81             |\n\n**Overfitting, Memorisation, and Generalisation**  \nTo assess overfitting to vendor identity, we conducted an ablation study by masking vendor names during both training and inference. This resulted in a 5% absolute decrease in F1 score (from 0.94 to 0.89), with the largest drops observed for vendors with high invoice counts. Additional regularisation strategies, such as dropout and label smoothing, were applied but yielded only marginal improvements (<0.01 F1). To further probe generalisation, we evaluated cross-client performance: when training on all but one client and testing on the held-out client, F1 dropped by 0.06 on average, indicating some sensitivity to client-specific patterns. Temporal drift was assessed by training on 2018\u20132021 data and testing on 2022\u20132023 invoices, resulting in a 0.03 F1 decrease. These findings suggest that while vendor and client information are strong predictive features, reliance on them may limit generalisability to new vendors, clients, or changing business practices. For real-world deployment, we recommend periodic retraining, monitoring for vendor and client drift, and exploring domain adaptation techniques such as adversarial training or feature disentanglement to mitigate overfitting.\n\n**Structured Input Analysis**"}}